{
 "name": "vertex_bevel_noop_divergence",
 "description": "A CAPABILITY GAP, not a law: the reference bevels a vertex and we do nothing at all. Our command answers `did not apply` and the mesh comes back byte-identical to its input, in 46 of the 57 vertex-bevel cells of the sweep; the two frozen here are two of them. The reference's rule is readable off the frozen numbers: each edge at the beveled vertex contributes a point at `inset` along it measured from the vertex, the vertex itself is removed, and every incident face is re-rung through those points -- at valence 3 a new triangle closes the corner. At a boundary corner of a folded quad (valence 2, inset 0.12) that is 4 vertices -> 5 and quad -> pentagon; at a valence-3 corner it is 5 vertices -> 7 and 2 faces -> 3. CLOSING THIS GAP MEANS IMPLEMENTING VERTEX BEVEL, not adjusting a constant.",
 "source": "Reference side: headless command-port capture of the vertex-bevel tool at inset 0.12 (dirty-geometry sweep, task 1123, cases vbev_pole_np_one / vbev_pole_triquad). vibe3d side: live drive of this build -- the command is rejected, which the fixture records with allowError.",
